1
00:00:00,340 --> 00:00:02,935
We have five tasks in this training
workspace.

2
00:00:03,115 --> 00:00:08,474
Explain a function, find a related note,
label a ticket, draw a training image, and

3
00:00:08,474 --> 00:00:10,218
turn a recording into text.

4
00:00:10,398 --> 00:00:12,353
Each expects a different result.

5
00:00:12,533 --> 00:00:17,124
Before looking at brands or version
numbers, ask what goes in and what must

6
00:00:17,124 --> 00:00:17,675
come out.

7
00:00:17,855 --> 00:00:21,973
That simple question prevents a surprising
amount of confusion.

8
00:00:23,423 --> 00:00:28,315
A language model can read the sample
function and generate a plain English

9
00:00:28,315 --> 00:00:29,109
explanation.

10
00:00:29,289 --> 00:00:33,097
It can also suggest a test or draft a
change when you ask.

11
00:00:33,277 --> 00:00:35,829
The output is text or code to review.

12
00:00:36,009 --> 00:00:40,309
For our validator, we still use the source
and tests to check the answer.

13
00:00:40,489 --> 00:00:44,989
The generated explanation helps us decide
what to inspect next.

14
00:00:46,465 --> 00:00:51,426
We can search a small set of synthetic
notes for the phrase zero item order.

15
00:00:51,606 --> 00:00:56,156
An embedding model makes a numerical
vector for each note and for the search

16
00:00:56,156 --> 00:00:56,575
phrase.

17
00:00:56,755 --> 00:01:01,095
A search system ranks nearby vectors and
returns likely matches.

18
00:01:01,275 --> 00:01:06,626
Retrieve and read the original note before
drawing a conclusion, because similarity

19
00:01:06,626 --> 00:01:08,624
only shows what may be related.

20
00:01:10,090 --> 00:01:15,050
A training triage queue uses three labels:
defect, feature, and question.

21
00:01:15,230 --> 00:01:19,911
The input says that zero item orders are
accepted, against a documented

22
00:01:19,911 --> 00:01:20,702
requirement.

23
00:01:20,882 --> 00:01:23,060
The output is the defect label.

24
00:01:23,240 --> 00:01:27,591
A dedicated classifier or a general model
can perform this task.

25
00:01:27,771 --> 00:01:32,827
The important design choice is the label
set and how you check it on new examples.

26
00:01:34,298 --> 00:01:36,338
Now look at three media tasks.

27
00:01:36,518 --> 00:01:40,433
A vision capable model can describe a
synthetic screenshot.

28
00:01:40,613 --> 00:01:44,476
An image generator can create a new
training illustration.

29
00:01:44,656 --> 00:01:48,315
A transcription model turns approved audio
into text.

30
00:01:48,495 --> 00:01:51,910
Some systems accept several modalities in
one model.

31
00:01:52,090 --> 00:01:57,501
Check the tool's input and output support
for the exact workflow before relying on

32
00:01:57,501 --> 00:01:57,699
it.

33
00:01:59,173 --> 00:02:02,075
One product may combine these
capabilities.

34
00:02:02,255 --> 00:02:07,416
Our search demo turns a question into an
embedding, retrieves a likely document,

35
00:02:07,416 --> 00:02:10,319
and passes that document to a language
model.

36
00:02:10,499 --> 00:02:14,967
The final answer is generated from text
supplied by the search step.

37
00:02:15,147 --> 00:02:18,157
Trace the steps on screen and check the
source link.

38
00:02:18,337 --> 00:02:22,435
This is a useful example of several model
types working together.

39
00:02:23,882 --> 00:02:25,902
Try three cards from this lesson.

40
00:02:26,082 --> 00:02:31,691
For explaining the validator, choose text
generation and verify the explanation with

41
00:02:31,691 --> 00:02:32,492
test output.

42
00:02:32,672 --> 00:02:37,896
For finding a related policy note, choose
embedding based retrieval and read the

43
00:02:37,896 --> 00:02:39,072
source it returns.

44
00:02:39,252 --> 00:02:43,746
For categorizing an issue, choose
classification and compare against

45
00:02:43,746 --> 00:02:45,662
examples labeled by a person.

46
00:02:45,842 --> 00:02:48,852
Write down the success check before
choosing the tool.
